INSERT INTO public.learn_articles(slug,title,excerpt,content,category_slug,author,read_time_minutes,status,is_featured,meta_title,meta_description,market,wall_market_code,wall_topic_id,wall_article_theme,wall_language_code,editorial_review_status,cover_image_url)
SELECT a.slug,a.title,a.excerpt,a.content,'surface-preparation','FRELUX Editorial',a.read_time_minutes,'draft',false,a.title,a.excerpt,'GB','GB',t.id,a.theme,'en','draft',a.cover_url
FROM (VALUES
  ('gb-timber-frame-drylining-overview', 'UK Timber Frame and Drylining: System Overview', 'A British overview of timber frame construction and its drylining chain: platform framing, sheathing, membranes, the plasterboard finishes, and why this light, fast system is shaping UK housebuilding.', $wallarticle1$# UK Timber Frame and Drylining: System Overview

Walk past a British housing site in the opening weeks of construction and the difference is immediate. Where a brick and block site still shows mortar boards and half-built leaves, the timber frame site shows a skeleton of studs rising in a single morning, straight, pale, and astonishingly complete. Within days the frame is sheathed, wrapped, and roofed, and the trades who follow work indoors, dry, and unhurried by the weather. This is the timber frame tradition, older than the cavity wall itself and now one of the fastest growing ways to build a house in the United Kingdom, and its finishing story is a chain the reader can hold in one sentence: a timber stud frame, wrapped against the weather, insulated between its studs, and lined inside with plasterboard finished to a smooth, paintable surface.

The system deserves an honest name before anything else. In its modern British form it is almost always a platform frame: storey-height panels of vertical timber studs, nailed through horizontal rails at top and bottom, built one floor at a time with each floor platform carrying the next. The panels arrive from a factory pre-assembled, openings for windows and doors already framed in, or in some builds the frame is cut and assembled on site from graded timber. The industry speaks of open panel frames, where the panels come with sheathing but insulation and services are fitted on site, and closed panel or cassette systems, where insulation, membranes, and sometimes even first-fix services are already built in. Each variation changes who does what, and when, but the anatomy the finisher inherits is the same: studs at regular centres, a sheathing layer stiffening the frame, a breather membrane outside resisting water while letting vapour escape, mineral wool or similar insulation between the studs, a vapour control layer on the warm side, a service zone for cables and pipes, and plasterboard fixed to the studs and finished smooth. NHBC''s chapter on external timber framed walls treats the whole build-up as one system with a defined sequence, and that is exactly how this article reads it [1].

Why a housebuilder chooses it is worth understanding, because the choice changes the finishing programme. The frame is light, so foundations can be lighter. It is fast, because a weatherproof shell can exist weeks before a masonry build reaches the same state, and every trade after the frame works protected from the weather. It is accurate, because factory-cut panels arrive dimensionally consistent in a way site carpentry rarely matches, and plasterboard linings reward that consistency. And it is dry: there is no wet plaster waiting weeks to cure, no water added to the building beyond what the trades bring, so the drying-out problems that haunt new masonry homes largely disappear. The NHBC trains site managers specifically in managing timber frame construction because the system rewards planning and punishes improvisation, a theme this series returns to in the installation article [2].

The reader meeting this system should see the frame as the substrate for everything inside. The inner face of the studs is where the dryliner works, fixing plasterboard sheets at centres the system specification defines, taping the joints or skimming the surface, and mist-coating before emulsion. The British Gypsum White Book, the specification manual the UK drylining industry works from, treats timber stud linings and partitions as engineered systems with tested build-ups, not as improvised carpentry, and the difference matters when fire resistance, sound insulation, or strength are part of the design [5]. A wall is no longer a wall: it is a tested assembly whose performance depends on the right board, the right fixings, the right joint treatment, in the right order.

The history gives the system its depth. Timber framing built much of Britain''s housing stock before brick became cheap, and the visible black and white elevations of Tudor towns are the same skeleton, exposed rather than hidden. Modern engineered timber frame re-entered British housebuilding with factory production, graded timber, and preservative treatment, and TRADA''s material specifications document the standards that brought it back: strength graded timber, moisture contents matched to the frame''s environment, sheathing and membranes with declared performances [3]. The revival is not nostalgia; it is precision manufacturing meeting a very old idea.

What this overview owes the rest of the series is a map. The materials article walks the reader through the shed: the graded studs, the boards, the membranes, the mineral wool, the screws and tapes, each with the question it answers. The installation article follows the sequence from frame erection to mist coat, the order in which a weatherproof, insulated, lined, and finished wall actually comes together. The quality article names the gates: frame alignment, moisture, membrane continuity, board fixing, joint quality. The maintenance article follows the finished wall through its life: the movement a timber frame makes as it dries and seasons, the cracks that matter and the ones that do not, and the repairs that respect the system beneath. Together they treat the timber frame wall as what it is: the most systematised way Britain currently builds a warm, dry, quickly finished home.

## The drylining chain, told as one sentence

If the overview had to shrink to a single sentence a reader could carry through the rest of the series, it would read: frame, sheathing, breather membrane, insulation, vapour control layer, plasterboard, jointing or skim, mist coat, emulsion. Each word in that chain is a material the materials article will put on a shelf, a step the installation article will put in order, a gate the quality article will check, and a liability the maintenance article will steward. The sentence has no optional parts; even the optional looking skim is a decision, and a decision is a fact recorded in the specification. What makes the timber frame tradition distinct in Britain is how tested that sentence is. The White Book families of lining and partition systems exist because the industry learned to sell confidence: a board, a fixing pattern, a joint treatment, and a finish, tested together and named as one system [5]. The reader who grasps that is already ahead of the householder who thinks a wall is board and hope.

## The system''s British geography

Timber frame''s share of British housebuilding has grown regionally and stubbornly, strongest in Scotland, where the tradition never fully left, and spreading through England as volume housebuilders learned what the factories could promise. The reasons are unsentimental. A single skilled on site workforce is harder to guarantee each year, while a factory in Warwickshire or Inverness can cut, assemble, and quality check a wall panel regardless of the weather outside it. Speed of weathertight enclosure, the single most schedule critical event in any build, is faster with timber than with masonry by weeks, and every week saved is capital cost saved. The NHBC''s decision to train managers specifically in managing timber frame construction, rather than treating it as carpentry with extra steps, is the clearest signal of the system''s arrival in the mainstream [2]. The overview''s honest geography ends where the reader''s own project begins, because whichever region the site sits in, the frame arrives with the same anatomy, and the finishing chain meets the same stud face.

## What the system is not, a boundary worth drawing

Three confusions follow the system around, and the overview clears them now. The first is the confusion with the half timbered elevations of old England: those beautiful black and white frames are visible structure, while a modern platform frame is a hidden skeleton behind finishes, built from graded, treated, engineered timber to a design the old framers would recognise as science. The second is the confusion with stud partitioning, the internal walls of every office fit out: a partition divides rooms, while a timber frame wall carries loads and excludes weather, and only the second is this series'' subject, though both end in plasterboard. The third is the confusion with masonry''s finishing habits, where wet plaster ruled for a century; the drylining chain is not masonry''s skim coat moved indoors, it is a different philosophy, dry, fast, and factory tested, whose jointing rules and board types come from a different literature. The reader who keeps the three boundaries straight reads every later article in the right light.

## Reading the system as the finisher meets it

Because this series is about finishing, the overview closes with the moment that belongs to the reader most of all: the morning the plasterboard arrives. The finisher stands in a room that is already warm, dry, and true, a privilege no wet-trade predecessor enjoyed, and the wall presents its stud face like a page of ruled paper awaiting print. What the finisher does next, screwing, taping, skimming, misting, belongs to the articles that follow. What the overview contributes is the knowledge that every board fixed that morning joins a story that began in a factory: graded timber cut to tolerance, a membrane jacket taped against the wind, insulation full to the stud depth, and a vapour layer sealed like a promise. The finisher is the last person who will ever see the wall''s bones, and the series'' whole ambition is that the reader finishes this topic as that person: informed, respectful, and quietly proud of the chain of hands behind the plasterboard.

## The standards'' shelf, where the system files its promises

The overview''s last duty is the map of the shelf where the system''s promises are written down. At the top sits the design standard for timber structures, the Eurocode the structural engineer works to, which the reader meets through the project drawings rather than directly. Beside it sits the NHBC chapter on external timber framed walls, the warranty world''s translation of engineering into site rules, the document a site manager can hold a delivery to [1]. Below it, the manufacturer literature forms the practical layer: TRADA''s material specifications for the frame''s timbers and boards, the White Book for the lining systems, and the Thistle guides for the plaster finishes, each naming tested products in tested combinations [3][5][7]. The building standards handbooks of Scotland and the approved documents south of the border sit beneath all of it as the regulatory floor, the level below which nothing may fall. The reader does not need to own this shelf; the overview asks only that the reader knows it exists, and that every sentence in the articles that follow can be traced to one of its ledgers.

## A closing paragraph for the reader with a decision pending

Some readers arrive at this overview with a plot, an architect''s appointment, and a decision pending between two ways of building. For that reader the overview ends with the honest comparison, not as advocacy but as respect. Masonry builds slowly and wet, carries thermal mass, and forgives the improvised site; timber frame builds fast and dry, carries precision, and forgives nothing but rewards discipline. The finishing chains differ accordingly: plaster on wet blockwork versus drylining on studs, curing schedules versus weathertight weeks. Neither tradition is wrong, and the strongest British builders quote both without prejudice, but the choice changes the reader''s next five years, and this series exists because the timber frame choice deserves to be made with its finishing story understood rather than discovered.

## References

1. NHBC, *Standards Part 6, Chapter 6.2: External timber framed walls* [1](https://www.thenbs.com/PublicationIndex/documents/details?Pub=NHBC&DocId=293688).
2. NHBC, *Managing timber frame construction* [2](https://www.nhbc.co.uk/training/course-index/managing-timber-frame-construction).
3. TRADA, *Timber frame building: materials specification*, Wood Information Sheet [3](https://turnertimber.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2017/03/TIMBER-FRAME-BUILDING-MATERIAL-SPECIFICATION.pdf).
4. Merron Brook, *Timber frame construction*, pocket guide [4](https://www.merronbrook.co.uk/assets/downloads/timber-frames/timberframepocketguide-aug2016.pdf).
5. British Gypsum, *White Book: Internal Partitions and Walls* [5](https://www.british-gypsum.com/specification/white-book-specification-selector/internal-partitions-walls).
6. British Gypsum, *Gyproc WallBoard 12.5 mm, product data sheet* [6](https://www.british-gypsum.com/documents/product-data-sheet-pds/british-gypsum-pds-gyproc-wallboard-12-5mm.pdf).
7. British Gypsum, *Thistle Plaster Systems PL0029* [7](https://www.british-gypsum.com/Specification/White-Book-Specification-Selector/plasters/thistle-plaster-systems/pl0029).
8. Scottish Government, *Building standards technical handbook 2017: domestic* [8](https://www.gov.scot/publications/building-standards-2017-domestic/0-general/08-durability-workmanship-and-fitness-of-materials/).

## Image credit

The cover photograph shows a timber-framed building under construction on Far Gosford Street, Coventry, England. Photograph by A J Paxton, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. A real photograph, not AI generated.$wallarticle1$, 9, 'overview', 'https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/Timber-framed_building_under_construction%2C_Far_Gosford_Street_-_geograph.org.uk_-_6844011.jpg/1280px-Timber-framed_building_under_construction%2C_Far_Gosford_Street_-_geograph.org.uk_-_6844011.jpg')
, ('gb-timber-frame-drylining-materials', 'Materials for UK Timber Frame and Drylining', 'A British materials guide to the timber frame shed: graded studs, sheathing, breather membranes, mineral wool, vapour control, plasterboards, screws and tapes, each chosen for the role it plays.', $wallarticle2$# Materials for UK Timber Frame and Drylining

The materials of a timber frame wall read like a well organised shed, and each item answers a question the wall will be asked. The studs carry the load. The sheathing stops the frame racking. The breather membrane keeps rain out while letting vapour through. The insulation slows the heat. The vapour control layer keeps warm indoor moisture out of the construction. The plasterboard makes the ceiling and walls smooth, fire safe, and ready for paint. The screws, tapes, and jointing compounds hold the linings together and hide the joints. Buy them as a system, and the wall performs as tested. Buy them as a shopping list of cheapest items, and the wall disappoints for decades.

The frame itself starts with graded timber. British site practice works with strength graded softwood, commonly C16 and C24, the numbers declaring the strength class the design relies on, kiln dried so the moisture content suits a heated building. The studs arrive treated against insects and decay where the specification calls for it, and the honest rule from TRADA''s materials specification is that the grade, the treatment, and the moisture content are not negotiable items; they are the structural signature of the frame [3]. Openings gain extra members around them, and the rails and studs meet in factory cut joints that a site carpenter assembling the frame can close without improvisation. The pocket guide Merron Brook publishes for its frames makes the same point in plainer words: the project specification, alongside the NHBC Standards or warranty technical manual, governs what is acceptable on any particular site [4].

Outside the studs comes the sheathing, a board layer that does the quiet structural work of stopping the frame parallelogramming under wind. The breather membrane that follows is the wall''s jacket: water resistant on the outside face, vapour open in the other direction, taped at its seams so wind cannot wash insulation performance away behind it. Neither layer is decorative, and both fail invisibly when wrong, which is why the quality article inspects them before anything covers them up.

Between the studs sits insulation, and in the UK that usually means mineral wool, friction fitted between the studs so there are no idle gaps. The insulation''s thickness is a design decision tied to the target U-value, not an installer preference, and it stops at the cavity the frame leaves around windows and other openings. The warm side then receives the vapour control layer, a continuous membrane or board facing whose single job is stopping heated air''s moisture from condensing inside the wall. Its continuity, lapped, taped, and sealed around every socket and pipe that pierces it, is the difference between a wall that ages well and one that quietly rots, and the Scottish technical handbook''s workmanship expectations are the regulatory voice behind that discipline [8].

The drylining aisle is where most readers start, so it deserves the most careful shelf. Standard plasterboard, such as the Gyproc WallBoard documented in British Gypsum''s product data sheets, is the workhorse: a gypsum core with paper faces, in 12.5 mm thickness as the common default, cuttable with a knife and a straight edge, and suitable for direct decoration or a skim [6]. Beside it sit the specialist boards the specification may call for: moisture resistant boards for bathrooms and kitchens, fire resistant boards where the tested system demands them, and sound rated boards for the party walls between dwellings, each named for the question it answers. The board is fixed to the studs with drywall screws at centres the tested system defines, and the joints are filled with jointing compound over joint tape, either finished to a paint ready standard or handed to the plasterer for a skim coat of finishing plaster. The Thistle plaster systems guide treats that choice, taping or skimming, as a genuine system decision with provenance, not a matter of taste [7].

The small parts hold the system together and deserve their own paragraph. Screws must be the fine threaded type the boards'' manufacturers specify, driven so the head just breaks the paper without tearing it. Joint tape, whether paper or self adhesive fibreglass, bridges the joints. Jointing compound comes as setting or drying types, each with its working window. Edge beads and skim profiles give thin plaster its defined edges at openings and external angles. Adhesive dabs, where a masonry background rather than a timber stud receives the board, are a different fixing method for a different wall, and this article keeps them in their own place. The British Gypsum White Book is the shelf''s reference: it lists the partition and lining systems with their tested performances, so a wall specified for sixty minutes of fire resistance or a defined sound rating is built from boards, fixings, and joint treatments that were tested together, in that combination [5].

Storage is the last material skill, because timber frame materials are unforgiving of neglect. Studs and boards must stay dry under cover, stacked flat on bearers; damp stored plasterboard warps, and wet graded timber can exceed the moisture content the design assumed. Membranes and tapes degrade under prolonged sunlight. Mineral wool is unbothered by weather but not by compression. The materials list is finished only when the delivery schedule matches the build sequence, so nothing sits in the weather waiting for its turn.

## The fixing shelf, and the choice it quietly makes

The screws and adhesives shelf is small, and it quietly decides the wall''s fate. Timber stud work takes fine threaded drywall screws, driven by a driver set to stop the head just below the paper face; the thread grips timber, and the head setting is a skill worth practising on offcuts, because a torn face cannot be repaired, only patched. The alternative fixings, nails driven by powder or gas tools on some sites, trade speed for a lower grip, and the specification decides between them, not the installer''s preference. Around openings and at external angles, edge beads and corner profiles give the thin finishes their straight lines, and the bead shelf rewards matching the bead to the finish: a skimmed wall takes one profile, a taped and jointed wall takes another. None of these parts carries the wall alone, and the materials article''s honest rule is that every item on the fixing shelf is chosen by asking which tested system the wall claims to be, because the system''s name is the only thing that makes a collection of parts into a wall with promises [5].

## The protective shelf, the wall''s quiet half

Two membranes protect the timber frame wall from its two enemies, and they belong in the same paragraph because they are so often confused. The breather membrane on the cold side resists liquid water and passes vapour, a seemingly impossible combination achieved by materials science and careful detailing; it laps so each course sheds the rain it sheds, and its tapes close the seams so wind cannot find the insulation. The vapour control layer on the warm side does the opposite job by opposite means: it is essentially airtight and vapour closed, so the moist air of a heated house cannot carry its cargo into the construction and drop it as condensation on the first cold surface. The two membranes together define the wall''s moisture strategy, and the materials article''s sternest sentence is that they are not interchangeable, not substitutable by the cheaper item the merchant happens to have, and not forgiving of the wrong tape [8]. Between them sits the insulation, mineral wool in most UK frames, and its material question is fullness: a stud depth fully filled, without the gaps behind and beside that halve the insulation''s real performance.

## The shelf''s single test, asked honestly

The materials article closes with the test that governs every purchase: does each item name the system it belongs to? A board without its system''s fixing pattern is a sheet of gypsum; a membrane without its tape is a jacket with open seams; a screw without its tested pattern is a fastener with opinions. The specification the designer wrote, and the White Book family it cites, is the only honest answer key, and the reader who buys to that answer builds a wall whose performance can be stated rather than guessed [5].

## The buyer''s closing honesty

The materials guide ends by returning to the buyer holding the list, because the shed''s lesson is a discipline of mind. Every item on the list exists because somewhere a wall failed without it, and every system name on the list exists because somewhere a collection of parts was tested until it earned promises. The buyer''s honest work is to name the system the wall claims to be, then buy its parts in that name, resisting both the cheaper substitution that saves a morning and costs a decade, and the expensive improvisation that adds nothing the test measured. The reader who ends this article slightly suspicious of unlabeled parts has read it correctly, because suspicion, in the materials shed, is just quality control wearing its weekend clothes.

## The factory''s role, materials with a serial number

The materials story of modern UK timber frame is mostly a factory story, and the shed deserves to know it. Wall panels arrive with graded, treated timber cut to tolerance; sheathing is nailed on jigs rather than balanced on scaffolding; windows can arrive already glazed into their panels; and closed panel systems can arrive with insulation, membranes, and first fix services already built in. What the factory sells, underneath the speed, is evidence: grading stamps on every stud, treatment certificates with batch numbers, and a production line whose quality control is a matter of record. The site''s materials work then shifts from choosing to verifying, checking deliveries against the specification and protecting what arrives rather than manufacturing it, and the materials article''s factory paragraph is really a lesson in trust: the modern way of building has moved the shed indoors, and the reader who understands that reads the delivery lorry as the most important shelf of all [3].

## The moisture resistant and fire rated shelves, honesty about the specialists

Two specialist shelves complete the tour, and they repay honesty. The moisture resistant board, its gypsum core and faces treated against humidity, belongs in bathrooms, kitchens, and utility zones, and its green or colour coded face is a specification fact, not a decorator''s suggestion. The fire rated board, with glass fibre reinforced cores, belongs wherever the tested system demands it, most commonly on party walls and in the escape routes flats require, and the White Book''s fire tested systems are the literature that names the combinations and the minutes they hold [5]. The specialist shelves share a quiet warning: the board''s colour is not the performance, the system is, and a fire rated board fixed with the wrong pattern is a claim the certificate never made. The materials article''s closing advice is therefore the same for both shelves: name the system, buy its parts in its name, and let the tested minutes be the wall''s promises.

## References

1. TRADA, *Timber frame building: materials specification*, Wood Information Sheet [3](https://turnertimber.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2017/03/TIMBER-FRAME-BUILDING-MATERIAL-SPECIFICATION.pdf).
2. Merron Brook, *Timber frame construction*, pocket guide [4](https://www.merronbrook.co.uk/assets/downloads/timber-frames/timberframepocketguide-aug2016.pdf).
3. British Gypsum, *White Book: Internal Partitions and Walls* [5](https://www.british-gypsum.com/specification/white-book-specification-selector/internal-partitions-walls).
4. British Gypsum, *Gyproc WallBoard 12.5 mm, product data sheet* [6](https://www.british-gypsum.com/documents/product-data-sheet-pds/british-gypsum-pds-gyproc-wallboard-12-5mm.pdf).
5. British Gypsum, *Thistle Plaster Systems PL0029* [7](https://www.british-gypsum.com/Specification/White-Book-Specification-Selector/plasters/thistle-plaster-systems/pl0029).
6. Scottish Government, *Building standards technical handbook 2017: domestic* [8](https://www.gov.scot/publications/building-standards-2017-domestic/0-general/08-durability-workmanship-and-fitness-of-materials/).

## Image credit

The cover photograph shows a British Gypsum plasterboard plant in England. Photograph by derek dye, CC BY-SA 2.0, via Wikimedia Commons. A real photograph, not AI generated.$wallarticle2$, 10, 'materials', 'https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/British_Gypsum_Plasterboard_Plant_-_geograph.org.uk_-_2757134.jpg/1280px-British_Gypsum_Plasterboard_Plant_-_geograph.org.uk_-_2757134.jpg')
, ('gb-timber-frame-drylining-installation', 'Building UK Timber Frame Walls: Installation Guide', 'A British installation sequence from frame to mist coat: erecting the platform frame, sheathing and membranes, insulation and vapour control, plasterboard fixing, taping or skimming, and the handover to paint.', $wallarticle3$# Building UK Timber Frame Walls: Installation Guide

The installation story of a timber frame wall is a relay, and each runner must be in order. The frame goes up, square and braced. The sheathing and breather membrane close it against the weather. The windows and doors close it further. The first fix trades thread their cables and pipes through the studs'' service zones. Then the insulation goes in, the vapour control layer seals the warm side, the plasterboard follows, and the finishing coat closes the chain. Skip a leg, run two legs at once on the same piece of wall, and the error hides itself under the next layer, which is why the sequence is not advice but discipline.

Erection begins with the ground the frame will stand on: a level, damp proofed surface, because the timber must never begin its life wicking water from below. Factory built panels arrive marked, craned or manhandled into position over the fixings the design drew, plumbed, and temporarily braced while the frame finds its permanent stiffness. The NHBC''s guidance for external timber framed walls treats the frame''s squareness, the fixings between panels, and the holding down of the structure against wind as inspection points, not assumptions, and the site that respects them hands the finisher a frame that is true [1]. Storey follows storey in a platform build, each floor''s joists becoming the working platform for the next. The tolerance the dryliner inherits, studs plumb and faces true, is decided in these first days and cannot be adjusted later with jointing compound, a truth the White Book''s system thinking underlines: the plasterboard lining performs as tested only on a frame built as tested [5].

Sheathing follows the frame, fixed to the studs with the nail pattern the design specifies, and the breather membrane follows the sheathing, lapped the correct way so water sheds downward, taped at the vertical seams, and detailed at the openings so wind and rain meet a continuous jacket. The membrane''s integrity is the first of the system''s invisible virtues: a torn or untaped jacket lets wind wash through the insulation and can, in the worst cases, carry water toward the timber, and none of it is visible from inside once the boards are up.

The internal sequence begins with first fix services. Cables run through the studs'' pre-cut holes or through notches, pipes take their routes, and every penetration of the eventual vapour control layer is known before the layer exists, because sealing them afterwards is how the wall fails. The insulation then friction fits between studs, cut full and slightly generous so it fills the stud depth without gaps behind, beside, or above. The vapour control layer follows: lapped, taped at every seam, and sealed around every socket box, pipe, and cable that pierces it, so the warm moist air of a lived-in house cannot find the cold sheathing and condense there. The Merron Brook guide''s plain instruction, that the specification and warranty standards govern the site, is aimed precisely at this stage, where workmanship is invisible the moment the next layer lands [4].

Plasterboard fixing is the leg most readers picture, and its craft is mostly order. Boards are cut to break on stud centres, so every edge lands on timber. Screws, fine threaded and of the specified length, are driven at the centres the system defines, heads set just below the face without breaking the paper. Boards gain a small gap at the floor edge, and vertical joints fall on studs, never mid air. Corners and openings receive the beads the system specifies. The British Gypsum product data for standard wallboard is explicit that the board serves its tested purposes when handled and fixed per the system documents, and the installation article keeps that citation honest by repeating it [6]. Around this point the site chooses its finishing route: taping and jointing, where compound and tape fill and finish the joints to a paint ready standard, or a skim, where a thin coat of finishing plaster covers the whole surface and is trowelled to a polished smoothness. The Thistle systems guide documents both as proven system finishes with their own sequences, and the choice belongs to the specification, not the mood of the week [7].

The sequence closes with mist coating and the handover to decoration, which the finishing chain article in this series follows further. What the installation owes the reader, at its end, is the honest shape of the whole relay: a frame made true in a morning, closed against weather in a day, insulated, sealed, lined, and finished in weeks, by a chain of trades each handing the next a surface they did not have to correct. That is the quiet promise of the system, and every leg of the sequence exists to keep it.

## The fixing craft, up close

Plasterboard fixing rewards three habits, and the first is planning the sheet. Boards are cut so vertical edges break on stud centres, and the thoughtful fixer marks the stud positions on the board face before lifting it, because a board lifted twice is a board dropped once. The second habit is the screw order: boards are fixed from the centre outward so the sheet pulls flat against the studs instead of trapping a pillow of air in its middle, and the last screw lands nearest the floor last. The third habit is the head setting, felt through the driver rather than seen, each head seated just below the surface without tearing the paper envelope that gives the board its strength. The installer who treats every board as the last one anyone will see has already served the decorator, because the taping or skimming that follows only honours what the fixing made true. British Gypsum''s product literature carries the same discipline in its system language, and the installation article borrows its authority rather than inventing its own [6].

## The finishing choice, in practice

The route between taping and skimming is decided by the specification, but the installer still executes the choice, so the craft of both belongs here. Taping and jointing fills and finishes only the joints: three passes of compound over paper tape for the standard level, each pass wider and thinner than the last, sanded lightly between coats, until the joint disappears under its feathered edges. Skimming coats the whole surface: a thin, even layer of finishing plaster, trowelled on and polished as it sets, turning the whole wall into one continuous surface with no visible joints at all. Taping is faster and lighter, and it suits the painted finish most modern interiors want; skimming gives the harder, brighter surface traditional British taste still asks for in hallways and ceilings. The Thistle systems guide documents the plastering side as a system with its own backgrounds and sequences, and the site that follows it gets a surface that takes the mist coat evenly, which is the whole wall''s first act of decoration [7].

## The weather weeks, and the schedule that respects them

The installation sequence''s honest shape is only visible against the calendar. A timber frame shell can be weathertight within a fortnight of the frame arriving, wrapped, glazed, and roofed, while a masonry build on the neighbouring plot is still mixing its first lintel bedding. The finishers then work in a dry building, and their materials behave accordingly: jointing compounds dry evenly rather than patchily, skims set in their expected windows, and boards stay flat. The price of that speed is vigilance about the frame''s own moisture story, and the installation article''s sternest instruction is to respect the frame''s dryness as a resource: wet trades are kept away from the studwork, the frame is never used as a scaffold or a storage rack, and the building is closed against weather at every day''s end. The NHBC''s timber frame management training makes the same point with inspection points and stop lines; the installation article makes it with the installer''s own hands [2].

## The handoff to the decorator, the sequence''s last act

The installation article''s final paragraphs belong to the quiet handoff that ends every drylining job: the mist coat. A newly lined, taped, or skimmed wall is porous, and paint applied full bodied to a porous surface grips unevenly, flashes, and peels early. The mist coat, a thinned first coat of emulsion or the manufacturer''s purpose made equivalent, seals the surface uniformly and gives the decoration its honest foundation. The installation sequence ends, in other words, not with the finisher but with the wall made ready for the decorator, and the reader who executes that last small step properly closes a chain that began with a factory cut stud and ends with a living room ready for furniture. The finishing chain''s own article follows the paint further; the installation article simply insists that the paint receive a surface that deserves it, because every coat before it was labour spent making the mist coat possible.

## The sequenced wall, told as a week

Because the sequence reads best against the working week, the installation article can compress its own instructions into a schedule the reader can hold. Day one, the frame arrives and rises, braced and plumbed while the crane waits. Day two, sheathing closes the skeleton and the breather jacket goes on, taped at the seams. Day three, windows and doors land in their openings and the building becomes weatherproof, the moment the whole system exists to reach. The following days bring first fix, insulation fitted full between studs, and the vapour control layer sealed at every penetration, photographed before it disappears. Then the drylining days: boards fixed, joints taped or the skim floated, and the mist coat closing the sequence. The week tells the truth of the system, that the timber frame wall is not a series of trades waiting on each other''s cure times but a relay of dry operations, each leg prepared by the one before it, and the reader who plans to that rhythm rather than against it finishes with the calmest site in the neighbourhood.

## The openings, told honestly

Openings are where the sequence concentrates its care, and they deserve their own telling. A window in a timber frame wall meets a more complicated world than a window in masonry: the opening''s trimmer studs carry the load around the gap, the cavity the frame leaves must be insulated and sealed so the wall''s thermal and airtight lines continue through the opening rather than pausing at its edges, and the breather membrane must dress into the opening and meet the window''s own seals without a gap. The NHBC''s chapter on external timber framed walls treats openings as engineered junctions with drawn details, and the installation article''s rule is the same one that governs the rest of the sequence: build to the detail, not to the improvisation, because an opening''s small sins are the first place a wall''s performance leaks away [1]. The finisher inherits the opening''s consequences, so the sequence that closes it properly is finishing work done in advance.

